gm-method (a user reports 'the :PORT_A and :PORT_B versions of my app look different / should be one thing'): the overwhelmingly likely cause is NOT a code fork but ONE codebase run as MULTIPLE long-lived server processes, each of which froze its own served-asset / node_modules / build-cache snapshot at boot -- so an edit (or a bridged dependency patch) lands on disk but only a process (re)started AFTER the edit serves it. DECISIVE WITNESS, cheap and non-visual: md5 the SAME asset URL served by each port AND the on-disk file -- if the two ports are byte-identical to EACH OTHER but differ from disk, they are identically STALE (proves 'not a fork, just old processes'), not divergent. GOTCHA: a dev static server usually gzips, so compare `curl --compressed` (decompressed) bytes, not raw curl piped to md5 -- comparing gzipped-served vs raw-disk falsely reports a diff. Also grep the repo for the second port number: if it only appears as a PORT default in scripts/tooling, nothing binds it specially -- it is the same server on another port. THE FIX is structural, not a render diff-chase: (1) establish there is ONE server and the modes differ by a QUERY on the same origin (client picks in-Worker-server vs network-client from the query), not a second port; (2) add a single-instance guard (loud EADDRINUSE message + clean exit) so a second divergent instance can't be started silently; (3) confirm the static handler sends no-cache and reads current disk per request so a running instance never needs a restart to serve an edit (only a browser hard-refresh); (4) kill the stale processes, leave ONE fresh instance, and md5-verify it serves current disk; (5) delete/doc any second client path (a stale 'serverless singleplayer' file the docs still reference but nothing imports is itself a two-versions hazard). Content-hashed build/bake output dirs already make two instances converge on the same artifact; the residual drift is purely which process is fresh.